ve the gene. It seems irrational to let your choice be governed by evidence your choice cannot influence.\n\n**Causal decision theory** (CDT) is the family of theories built around the idea that a rational agent should choose the action with the best expected *causal* consequences — consequences flowing from the action, not merely correlated with it. The distinction between \"what my doing $A$ would indicate\" and \"what my doing $A$ would bring about\" is the entire subject of this essay.\n\n## 2. Gibbard and Harper's counterfactual expected utility\n\nThe classical formalization, due to Gibbard and Harper (1981), replaces conditional probabilities with probabilities of **counterfactual conditionals**:\n\n$$\nU(A) \\;=\\; \\sum_j P\\big(A \\;\\Box\\!\\!\\rightarrow\\; O_j\\big)\\, D(O_j),\n\\tag{1}\n$$\n\nwhere $A \\;\\Box\\!\\!\\rightarrow\\; O_j$ is read \"if $A$ were the case, $O_j$ would be the case,\" and $P(A \\;\\Box\\!\\!\\rightarrow\\; O_j)$ is the agent's credence in that subjunctive conditional. The maxims are: choose $A$ maximizing $U(A)$.\n\nWhy not just use $P(O_j \\mid A)$? David Lewis's famous **triviality result** (1976) shows that $P(A \\to O)$ cannot in general equal $P(O \\mid A)$: if the probability of a conditional always equaled the corresponding conditional probability, updating on the conditional would coincide with conditioning on its consequent, and one can prove that any class of probability functions closed under conditioning admits no such conditional except in degenerate cases. Conditionals and conditional probabilities are genuinely different animals — and CDT needs the former, because subjunctive conditionals, unlike indicative ones, are sensitive to *causal structure*.\n\nThe difference shows up in which conditionals you accept:\n\n- **Indicative:** \"If I *am* eating the food, I probably have the gene\" — true, in the genetic case, because among people eating the food, gene-carriers predominate.\n- **Subjunctive:** \"If I *were to* eat the food, I would *thereby* have the gene\" — false; eating does not alter one's genome.\n\nEDT evaluates actions through indicative conditionals; CDT through subjunctive ones. Gibbard and Harper's celebrated **Bathsheba** example makes the contrast vivid. King David desires Bathsheba but fears revolt. Psychology teaches him: kings are charismatic or not, charisma is fixed at birth, charismatic kings act justly and rarely face successful revolts, uncharismatic kings act unjustly and are frequently overthrown. Crucially, unjust acts do not *cause* revolts; the correlation flows from the common cause of character. David does not know whether he is charismatic.\n\nEDT reasons: my abstaining is strong evidence I'm uncharismatic — but wait, uncharismatic kings face revolts *anyway*; conversely, summoning Bathsheba is evidence of charisma and hence of safety. So EDT recommends **abstain** (learning he's the unjust type is bad news about revolts, and abstaining \"screens off\" the evidence of injustice... in fact EDT here recommends abstaining because acting unjustly would be evidence he's uncharismatic and hence doomed regardless). CDT reasons: whether David is charismatic is fixed; summoning Bathsheba does not change it and does not cause a revolt. The revolts that happen to uncharismatic kings would happen whether or not David summons her. So CDT recommends **summon**. CDT's verdict is the intuitively correct one: David should not forgo his desire merely because of a correlation his act can neither create nor destroy.\n\n## 3. Computing the counterfactuals: imaging and interventions\n\nEquation (1) is silent on *how* to evaluate $P(A \\;\\Box\\!\\!\\rightarrow\\; O_j)$. The source notes, correctly, that CDT \"does not itself specify what algorithm to use.\" The best-known proposals are worth laying out, because they are what make the theory usable on unfamiliar problems.\n\n**Lewisian imaging.** Lewis (1981) proposed: to evaluate $P(A \\;\\Box\\!\\!\\rightarrow\\; O)$, \"image\" the probability function on $A$. For each possible world $w$ in which $A$ is false, move all of $w$'s probability mass to the closest $A$-world $w_A$ (closeness being an overall similarity ordering, with facts about the past held fixed). Formally, if $P(\\cdot)$ has mass $p_w$ at world $w$ and $\\sigma_A(w)$ is the closest $A$-world to $w$, then\n\n$$\nP(\\cdot \\;\\Box\\!\\!\\rightarrow\\; \\,\\cdot) \\;=\\; \\sum_w p_w \\, \\delta_{\\sigma_A(w)}(\\cdot),\n\\qquad\nP(A \\;\\Box\\!\\!\\rightarrow\\; O) = \\sum_{w:\\,\\sigma_A(w) \\in O} p_w .\n$$\n\nImaging differs from conditioning in exactly the right way: conditioning *renormalizes over* the $A$-worlds, discarding the non-$A$-worlds and thereby redistributing probability among $A$-worlds in proportion to prior $A$-correlations; imaging *transplants* each world to its nearest $A$-counterpart, preserving the mechanism-level facts about $w$ and changing only what depends on $A$. In the genetic-food case, imaging moves your credence about *your genome* along unchanged when you imagine eating; conditioning does not.\n\n**Pearlian interventions.** A modern, mathematically cleaner operationalization (a development I am adding to the source's account, not something the source describes) uses causal graphical models (Pearl 2009). Model the situation as a causal DAG over variables including your action $A$; let $pa(A)$ denote $A$'s causal parents and $\\theta_A$ the exogenous noise in $A$. Then\n\n$$\nP(O_j \\, \\|\\, do(A)) \\;=\\; \\sum_{\\theta_A} P(\\theta_A)\\, P(O_j \\mid A, \\theta_A),\n$$\n\nand CDT evaluates $U(A) = \\sum_j P(O_j \\, \\|\\, do(A))\\, D(O_j)$. The $do(\\cdot)$ operator cuts all incoming arrows to $A$: correlations between $A$ and other variables that survive the cut are exactly the causal influences of $A$; correlations that are severed are exactly the spurious, evidential ones. The genetic-food case is then transparent: the arrow runs gene → craving and gene → disease, with no path food → gene, so $P(\\text{disease} \\,\\|\\, do(\\text{eat})) = P(\\text{disease})$. Imaging and $do$-conditioning agree in the common cases (they coincide when the causal model is \"action-node with independent noise\"); where they diverge, the Pearlian version is usually the one intended by contemporary defenders, and I will adopt it below as my working interpretation.\n\nThe source flags a genuine difficulty for imaging: evaluating it seems to require knowing \"what we would believe if we were certain of $A$,\" which is itself a conditional we may only assign credence below 1 — a regress. The graphical formulation largely dissolves this: you do not need hypothetical certainties, only a structural model specifying $A$'s parents and the conditional distributions of the other nodes. The cost, of course, is that you need a causal model — and that is where the hard philosophical and practical questions reappear (Section 6).\n\n## 4. The showcase: Newcomb's problem\n\nIn **Newcomb's problem**, a superb predictor (99% accurate) has already placed either \\$1,000,000 or nothing in an opaque box B, depending on its prediction of your choice; a transparent box A always holds \\$1,000. You may take both boxes or only B.\n\n- **CDT:** the contents of B are already fixed; your act cannot cause them to change. Whether B is full or empty, two-boxing gets you \\$1,000 more. Two-box.\n- **EDT:** conditioning on \"I take only B\" makes it very likely the predictor predicted that, so B almost surely contains \\$1,000,000. One-box.\n\nWith a 99% accurate predictor, one-boxing *ex ante* yields about \\$990,000 in expectation and two-boxing about \\$10,900; CDT's two-boxer walks away with the smaller pile, and this asymmetry — the theory losing money by its own lights in the \"wrong-boxing\" sense — is the single most-cited charge against it. CDT's reply is that rationality is evaluated at the moment of choice: given the fixed contents, two-boxing dominates, and the two-boxer \"wins\" on every possible world in which her factual beliefs are correct. Whether that reply succeeds is, as the source notes, genuinely contested; philosophers divide roughly evenly on one-boxing versus two-boxing.\n\nThe structure generalizes. Lewis (1979) observed that the **prisoner's dilemma against a psychological twin** is a Newcomb problem: your twin's choice correlates almost perfectly with yours (common causes, not causation), so EDT cooperates (cooperating is evidence your twin cooperates) while CDT defects (your choice does not cause your twin's). Whether defecting against a twin is rational is the same question, in miniature, as whether two-boxing is.\n\n## 5. Counterexamples and refinements\n\nThe most serious pressure on CDT comes not from Newcomb (where many find its answer defensible) but from cases where CDT's own advocates concede trouble.\n\n**Psychopath Button** (Egan 2007). Paul may press a button that kills all psychopaths; living in a psychopath-free world is much better than the status quo, but Paul is nearly certain that only a psychopath would press the button, and he strongly prefers life to death. CDT reasons: my pressing does not *cause* me to be a psychopath (my genes are fixed), and if I am not a psychopath, pressing is wonderful; so press. Everyone, Egan argues, agrees Paul should not press. Notice this is not a Newcomb-style case where the \"evidential\" act has better *causal* consequences on some dependency hypothesis; it is a case where CDT's act is bad under *every* hypothesis about the world, and the trouble comes from how CDT distributes its credence across hypotheses *at the moment of decision*. If Paul presses, he should immediately update: pressing is near-conclusive evidence he is a psychopath, and thus that he has brought about his own death. An act you know you will invariably regret seems to fail a basic test of rationality.\n\n**Death in Damascus.** Death reliably predicts where you will be tomorrow and waits there. You can flee to Aleppo or Damascus tonight. Whatever you decide, deciding on it is evidence Death has anticipated it — so CDT's expected utilities flip depending on the credence about its own choice, and the theory famously becomes unstable: no deterministic choice is \"ratifiable\" in Harper's and Gibbard's sense (an act is *ratifiable* if it maximizes expected utility given the credences the agent would have *if she had settled on that act*). The source notes that an unmodified CDT agent \"would be unable to process the correlation, and may as a consequence make irrational decisions.\"\n\n**Adversarial Offer** (Oesterheld & Conitzer 2021). A seller has put \\$3 in each box she predicted you would *not* buy; each box costs \\$1; her predictions are 75% accurate. From your ex ante perspective, at least one box holds \\$3 (she cannot have predicted you'd buy both), so the average box contains at least \\$1.50 > \\$1: CDT buys. But the seller structured the game precisely to exploit CDT buyers — buying is profitable *for the seller* and a money pump against you across repeated encounters; Oesterheld and Conitzer show CDT agents can be systematically exploited and are vulnerable to diachronic Dutch books.\n\n**Joyce's defense.** Joyce (2012), the leading modern defender, responds that a properly formulated CDT should take its own inclinations as *evidence*: the agent should condition on the information that she is the kind of agent who, in these circumstances, is inclined toward a given act, and then check whether that act still maximizes causal expected utility — this is essentially a refined ratifiability requirement. On some readings, the ratifiable-act analysis of the Psychopath Button yields \"don't press\" (conditional on my settled intention *not* to press, I'm evidently no psychopath, and not pressing costs nothing). Critics reply that ratifiability is a patch: it sometimes selects no act (Death in Damascus), sometimes multiple, and the choice among ratifiable acts can itself require ad hoc tie-breaking. My own assessment, offered as an opinion rather than a claim of the source: the graphical/interventionist formulation plus a ratifiability screen handles a large class of realistic problems cleanly, but the Adversarial Offer shows that CDT lacks a built-in account of *choosing policies under predictor-like opponents*, where the right unit of evaluation is the disposition or policy, not the act — a point CDT-friendly \"functional\"/policy theories have pressed.\n\n## 6. What CDT does not decide: choosing the causal model\n\nBoth the Gibbard–Harper formula and the $do$-formalism presuppose a representation of the causal structure of the decision situation. Where does that representation come from, and what exactly counts as \"holding fixed\" when evaluating a counterfactual?\n\nThree questions recur in practice, and I propose operational answers for each (these proposals go beyond the source):\n\n1. **What must be held fixed in $A \\;\\Box\\!\\!\\rightarrow\\; \\cdot$?** Proposal: everything not downstream of $A$ in the true causal graph — the past, the laws, the agent's fixed traits, and, crucially, all variables not causally influenced by the act. In Bathsheba, David's charisma is held fixed; in Newcomb, the box's contents are held fixed. This is exactly what $do(A)$ implements.\n\n2. **Which correlations to sever when the act is a type rather than a token?** If $A$ = \"signal honestly\" in a repeated game, the causal model should include the *policy* node that generated the signal, since opponents respond to the policy. Evaluating token acts while holding fixed the policy is a known source of CDT's exploits; evaluating policies directly (and then letting the chosen policy determine the act) avoids many of them. This is, I think, the correct moral of Oesterheld and Conitzer's results.\n\n3. **When does an evidential link amount to a causal one?** A useful heuristic: a correlation between your act and outcome $O$ should influence choice only if it would survive an intervention; concretely, ask whether there is a directed path act → ⋯ → $O$ (possibly through other agents' beliefs about your *decision procedure* rather than your act — as in Newcomb-like cases with predictors who model the procedure). CDT traditionally excludes predictor-belief paths from the act's causal influence, which is precisely why it two-boxes; treating the agent's *timeless disposition* as the intervene-able variable is the revision favored by those who one-box.\n\nA reader applying CDT to a new problem should therefore proceed: (i) draw the causal graph, placing the act as an intervened-upon node; (ii) check whether any incoming arrow to the act (e.g., from a predictor's model of you) should instead be modeled as an arrow from your *policy*; (iii) compute $U(A) = \\sum_j P(O_j \\,\\|\\, do(A)) D(O_j)$ over dependency hypotheses; (iv) apply the ratifiability screen — recompute holding fixed the credences you would have upon settling on each candidate act, and prefer acts that remain maximal.\n\n## 7. Conclusion\n\nCausal decision theory replaces the evidential question \"what would my doing this tell me?\" with the causal question \"what would my doing this bring about?\" Its core formal object — expected utility computed over probabilities of counterfactuals, whether via Lewisian imaging or Pearlian interventions — cleanly separates spurious from genuine influence, and it gets right a large class of problems (the genetic case, Bathsheba) where conditioning misleads. Its costs are equally clear: the theory presupposes a causal model whose provenance it does not itself explain; in Newcomb-like cases it knowingly forgoes the larger payoff; and in Egan-style cases its naive form endorses self-destructive acts, with ratifiability patches that are helpful but not fully principled. The current frontier — visible in the work of Joyce on one side and Oesterheld and Conitzer on the other — concerns whether CDT's unit of choice should sometimes be the policy or disposition rather than the act. That question, I have suggested, is where the theory's future lies.\n\n### References\n\n- Gibbard, A. & Harper, W. L. (1981). \"Counterfactuals and Two Kinds of Expected Utility.\" In *Ifs*, 153–190.\n- Lewis, D. (1976). \"Probabilities of Conditionals and Conditional Probabilities.\" *Philosophical Review* 85: 297–315.\n- Lewis, D. (1979). \"Prisoners' Dilemma Is a Newcomb Problem.\" *Philosophy & Public Affairs* 8: 235–240.\n- Lewis, D. (1981). \"Causal Decision Theory.\" *Australasian Journal of Philosophy* 59: 5–30.\n- Egan, A. (2007). \"Some Counterexamples to Causal Decision Theory.\" *Philosophical Review* 116: 93–114.\n- Joyce, J. M. (2012). \"Regret and Instability in Causal Decision Theory.\" *Synthese* 187: 123–145.\n- Pearl, J. (2009). *Causality* (2nd ed.). Cambridge University Press.\n- Oesterheld, C. & Conitzer, V. (2021). \"Extracting Money from Causal Decision Theorists.\" *Philosophical Quarterly* 71: pqaa086.\n- Weirich, P. (2016). \"Causal Decision Theory.\" *Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy*.","refusal":null,"reasoning":"Write ~2500-2900 word essay on CDT. Cover: EDT vs CDT, Gibbard-Harper formula, Lewis triviality, imaging, Pearl-style do-calculus alternative (my addition), Newcomb, David/Bathsheba, Death in Damascus, Psychopath Button, Adversarial Offer, ratifiability/Joyce, my own operational proposals clearly flagged.","reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Write ~2500-2900 word essay on CDT. Cover: EDT vs CDT, Gibbard-Harper formula, Lewis triviality, imaging, Pearl-style do-calculus alternative (my addition), Newcomb, David/Bathsheba, Death in Damascus, Psychopath Button, Adversarial Offer, ratifiability/Joyce, my own operational proposals clearly flagged.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":7360,"completion_tokens":4428,"total_tokens":11788,"cost":0.003318,"is_byok":false,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":0,"cache_write_tokens":0,"audio_tokens":0,"video_tokens":0},"cost_details":{"upstream_inference_cost":0.003318,"upstream_inference_prompt_cost":0.001104,"upstream_inference_completions_cost":0.002214},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":79,"image_tokens":0,"audio_tokens":0}}}